<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Lint configuration for the Android app.

  The rule that matters: the vendored CW decoder is exempt.
  `morsekit/cw/` is a line-faithful Kotlin port of the C99 core vendored into the
  iOS app, itself byte-identical to the Carrier Wave firmware's host-tested copy
  (see PROVENANCE.md in that directory). Keeping it diffable against its source
  is worth more than any style opinion lint could have about it, and CLAUDE.md
  says not to reformat, relicense, tidy or relocate it. So lint says nothing
  about that package — a finding there would be an invitation to break the
  provenance.

  Everything else is deliberately left at lint's defaults. Suppress an issue
  here only with a comment saying why, so the file stays a record of decisions
  rather than a place findings go to be forgotten.
-->
<lint>
    <issue id="all">
        <ignore path="src/main/java/app/anothermorsetrainer/morsekit/cw" />
    </issue>

    <!--
      targetSdk deliberately trails compileSdk by one (36 against 37, see the
      comment on it in build.gradle.kts): the AGP 9 / compileSdk 37 move was a
      toolchain change, and raising targetSdk changes the app's behaviour on
      Android 17, which nothing in CI can run. Remove this when targetSdk moves;
      Play's target-API deadline for 37 is the one that forces it.
    -->
    <issue id="OldTargetApi" severity="ignore" />

    <!--
      Lint 9.x reports newer *major* versions too (NewerVersionAvailable, which
      GradleDependency does not cover). OkHttp 5 is one: a major with API changes
      behind the one HTTP fetch this app makes (NewsFetcher), and no test runs
      that path. Moving it is a change to read the 5.x migration notes for, not
      a version-string edit in a toolchain PR. Only okhttp is silenced; every
      other dependency is still checked.
    -->
    <issue id="NewerVersionAvailable">
        <ignore regexp="okhttp" />
    </issue>
</lint>
